                <pb facs="0133" n="125" sameAs="frus:frus1937v04/pg125"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">793.94 Conference/138: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of
                            State to the Ambassador in Japan</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Grew</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1937-10-30T19:00:00-05:00">October 30, 1937—7
                        p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>276. Your 503, October 30, 4 p.m. Inasmuch as news reports and <hi
                        rendition="#italic">ballons d’essais</hi> concerning Japanese attitude may
                    be frequent in the coming days and as the Department desires to have as
                    authoritative information as practicable, the Department desires that you use
                    your discretion as to making informal inquiries of the Government in regard to
                    any such reports, including the report under reference. With <pb facs="0134"
                        n="126" xml:id="pg126"/> further reference to the latter report, the
                    Department would suggest that you make no special and separate approach but take
                    occasion to make inquiry in regard thereto when you are approaching the Foreign
                    Office on some other matter.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Welles</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
